<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<exercises xml:id="sec-6-12-exercises" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Programming Projects</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The following projects require you to design, implement, and verify complete Java
      classes in your local development environment (IDE). Each project emphasizes strict
      encapsulation, explicit class invariants, defensive input validation, and automated
      verification using the Arrange–Act–Assert pattern with Java assertions (<c>-ea</c>).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 1: DoughBatch                                        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-dough-batch">
    <title>Artisan Dough Batch Tracker</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In professional baking, formulas are expressed as percentages relative to total flour
        weight (baker's percentages). Hydration—the ratio of water mass to flour mass—is the
        single most important factor governing dough extensibility, crumb structure, and baking
        characteristics.
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement an immutable class named <c>DoughBatch</c> that tracks flour,
        water, and salt masses in grams, calculates hydration percentage, and supports scaling.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-dough-batch">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>DoughBatch</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>DoughBatch</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- flourGrams : double</p>
              <p>- waterGrams : double</p>
              <p>- saltGrams : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ DoughBatch(flourGrams : double, waterGrams : double, saltGrams : double)</p>
              <p>+ getFlourGrams() : double</p>
              <p>+ getWaterGrams() : double</p>
              <p>+ getSaltGrams() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateHydration() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateTotalMass() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateSaltPercentage() : double</p>
              <p>+ scale(factor : double) : DoughBatch</p>
              <p>+ equals(obj : Object) : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>flourGrams &gt; 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>waterGrams &gt; 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>saltGrams &gt;= 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>calculateHydration()</c> returns <m>(\text{waterGrams} / \text{flourGrams}) \times 100.0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>scale(factor)</c> returns a <em>new</em> <c>DoughBatch</c> with all ingredient
            masses multiplied by <c>factor</c>. The factor must be strictly positive (<c>factor &gt; 0.0</c>).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Override <c>equals(Object)</c> using <c>Double.compare()</c> with an epsilon tolerance
            of <m>10^{-4}</m> grams.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write an Arrange–Act–Assert test class named <c>DoughBatchTest</c> verifying construction,
        hydration math, scaling immutability, and rejection of non-positive flour weights.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Notice that <c>scale()</c> returns a new instance rather than modifying existing fields.
        This preserves immutability, similar to how Java's <c>String.toUpperCase()</c> works.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 2: CharacterAttribute                                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-character-attribute">
    <title>Role-Playing Game Character Attribute</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In role-playing games (RPGs), character attributes (such as Strength, Agility, or Wisdom)
        have a permanent base score and dynamic temporary modifiers (buffs and debuffs from spells,
        equipment, or injuries). The effective score is clamped within standard game bounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement a class named <c>CharacterAttribute</c> that manages a single
        attribute's state.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-character-attribute">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>CharacterAttribute</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>CharacterAttribute</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- name : String</p>
              <p>- baseValue : int</p>
              <p>- modifier : int</p>
              <p>- minAllowed : int</p>
              <p>- maxAllowed : int</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ CharacterAttribute(name : String, baseValue : int, minAllowed : int, maxAllowed : int)</p>
              <p>+ CharacterAttribute(name : String, baseValue : int)</p>
              <p>+ getName() : String</p>
              <p>+ getBaseValue() : int</p>
              <p>+ getModifier() : int</p>
              <p>+ getEffectiveValue() : int</p>
              <p>+ applyModifier(delta : int) : void</p>
              <p>+ resetModifier() : void</p>
              <p>+ permanentlyIncreaseBase(delta : int) : void</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>name != null &amp;&amp; !name.trim().isEmpty()</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>minAllowed &lt; maxAllowed</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>baseValue &gt;= minAllowed &amp;&amp; baseValue &lt;= maxAllowed</c></p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The 2-parameter constructor defaults <c>minAllowed = 1</c> and <c>maxAllowed = 100</c>
            using explicit constructor chaining (<c>this(...)</c>).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>getEffectiveValue()</c> returns <c>baseValue + modifier</c>, clamped so it never
            falls below <c>minAllowed</c> or exceeds <c>maxAllowed</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write a test suite in <c>CharacterAttributeTest</c> verifying that extreme negative
        modifiers clamp to <c>minAllowed</c> and extreme positive buffs clamp to <c>maxAllowed</c>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Use <c>Math.max(minAllowed, Math.min(maxAllowed, baseValue + modifier))</c> to implement
        clamping cleanly in one line.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 3: RoomRenovation                                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-room-renovation">
    <title>Room Renovation and Flooring Estimator</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Contractors estimating interior remodeling must calculate both floor surface area
        (for tile or hardwood flooring) and perimeter wall length (for baseboard molding),
        adding standard industry waste allowances.
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement a class named <c>RoomRenovation</c> that models a rectangular room.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-room-renovation">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>RoomRenovation</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>RoomRenovation</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- roomLabel : String</p>
              <p>- lengthFeet : double</p>
              <p>- widthFeet : double</p>
              <p>- flooringUnitCost : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ RoomRenovation(roomLabel : String, lengthFeet : double, widthFeet : double, flooringUnitCost : double)</p>
              <p>+ getRoomLabel() : String</p>
              <p>+ getLengthFeet() : double</p>
              <p>+ getWidthFeet() : double</p>
              <p>+ getFlooringUnitCost() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateFloorArea() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculatePerimeter() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateFlooringCost(wasteAllowancePercent : double) : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateBaseboardCost(costPerLinearFoot : double) : double</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>roomLabel != null &amp;&amp; !roomLabel.trim().isEmpty()</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>lengthFeet &gt; 0.0 &amp;&amp; widthFeet &gt; 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>flooringUnitCost &gt;= 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>calculateFlooringCost(wastePercent)</c> asserts <c>wastePercent &gt;= 0.0</c> and computes:
            <md>\text{area} \times \left(1.0 + \frac{\text{wastePercent}}{100.0}\right) \times \text{flooringUnitCost}</md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write a verification program <c>RenovationDemo</c> that configures three rooms in a house,
        computes total material requirements with a 10% flooring waste factor, and displays an
        itemized bill of materials.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 4: RoofObstruction                                   -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-roof-obstruction">
    <title>Rooftop Solar Shading and Setback Calculator</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When designing residential rooftop solar panel arrays, engineers must account for shade
        cast by rooftop obstructions (such as chimneys, HVAC units, or parapets). The shadow
        length cast by an obstruction of height <m>h</m> when the sun is at elevation angle
        <m>\theta</m> is given by trigonometry:
        <md>L = \frac{h}{\tan(\theta)}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement a class named <c>RoofObstruction</c> to calculate minimum solar
        setback distances.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-roof-obstruction">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>RoofObstruction</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>RoofObstruction</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- identifier : String</p>
              <p>- heightInches : double</p>
              <p>- widthInches : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ RoofObstruction(identifier : String, heightInches : double, widthInches : double)</p>
              <p>+ getIdentifier() : String</p>
              <p>+ getHeightInches() : double</p>
              <p>+ getWidthInches() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateShadowLength(solarElevationDegrees : double) : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateBufferZoneArea(solarElevationDegrees : double) : double</p>
              <p>+ isPanelShaded(panelDistanceInches : double, solarElevationDegrees : double) : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>identifier != null &amp;&amp; !identifier.trim().isEmpty()</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>heightInches &gt; 0.0 &amp;&amp; widthInches &gt; 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>calculateShadowLength(solarElevationDegrees)</c> requires that <m>\theta</m> satisfy
            <c>solarElevationDegrees &gt; 0.0 &amp;&amp; solarElevationDegrees &lt; 90.0</c>.
            Convert the angle to radians via <c>Math.toRadians()</c> before calling <c>Math.tan()</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>isPanelShaded()</c> returns <c>true</c> if a solar panel placed at distance
            <c>panelDistanceInches</c> falls within the shadow length.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write unit tests in <c>RoofObstructionTest</c> testing winter solstice sun angles (e.g.,
        <m>22^\circ</m>) and summer solstice sun angles (e.g., <m>65^\circ</m>).
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Java's <c>Math.tan()</c> expects radians, not degrees. Always call
        <c>Math.toRadians(solarElevationDegrees)</c> first.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 5: MaritimeHorizon                                   -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-maritime-horizon">
    <title>Maritime Horizon and Lighthouse Range Calculator</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Because the Earth is curved, the distance an observer at sea can see before the surface
        drops below the horizon depends on the observer's eye height above sea level. Accounting
        for standard atmospheric refraction, the geometric distance to the horizon in nautical
        miles is modeled by:
        <md>d \approx 1.17 \times \sqrt{h}</md>
        where <m>h</m> is the height of eye in feet. When observing a raised target (such as a
        lighthouse lantern of height <m>H</m>), the maximum distance of geographical visibility
        is the sum of the observer's horizon distance and the target's horizon distance:
        <md>D_{\text{max}} = 1.17 \times \sqrt{h} + 1.17 \times \sqrt{H}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement a class named <c>MaritimeHorizon</c>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-maritime-horizon">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>MaritimeHorizon</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>MaritimeHorizon</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- vesselOrStationName : String</p>
              <p>- eyeHeightFeet : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ MaritimeHorizon(vesselOrStationName : String, eyeHeightFeet : double)</p>
              <p>+ getVesselOrStationName() : String</p>
              <p>+ getEyeHeightFeet() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateDistanceToHorizon() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateGeographicRange(target : MaritimeHorizon) : double</p>
              <p>+ canSightingOccur(target : MaritimeHorizon, separationDistanceNauticalMiles : double) : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>vesselOrStationName != null &amp;&amp; !vesselOrStationName.trim().isEmpty()</c></p></li>
        <li><p><c>eyeHeightFeet &gt;= 0.0</c></p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>calculateGeographicRange(target)</c> accepts another <c>MaritimeHorizon</c> instance,
            validates that <c>target != null</c>, and sums both horizon distances.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>canSightingOccur()</c> asserts that <c>separationDistanceNauticalMiles &gt;= 0.0</c>
            and returns whether the separation is less than or equal to the combined geographic range.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write a simulation program <c>MaritimeDemo</c> modeling a container ship bridge (height
        of eye 75 ft), a small sailboat cockpit (height of eye 9 ft), and a historic offshore
        lighthouse (focal plane height 130 ft).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 6: Star and Exoplanet                                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-star-exoplanet">
    <title>Exoplanet Orbital Period and Habitable Zone Model</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Astronomers discovering extrasolar planets use physical laws to characterize planetary
        orbits. By Kepler's Third Law, a planet's orbital period <m>T</m> (in Earth years)
        around a host star of mass <m>M</m> (in solar masses <m>M_\odot</m>) at semi-major axis
        <m>a</m> (in Astronomical Units, AU) is:
        <md>T = \sqrt{\frac{a^3}{M}}</md>
        A star's circumstellar habitable zone (the range of orbital distances where liquid water
        can exist) scales with the star's bolometric luminosity <m>L</m> (relative to our Sun):
        <md>r_{\text{inner}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{L}{1.1}}, \quad r_{\text{outer}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{L}{0.53}}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement two collaborating classes: <c>Star</c> and <c>Exoplanet</c>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-star">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>Star</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>Star</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- name : String</p>
              <p>- massSolarMasses : double</p>
              <p>- luminositySolar : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ Star(name : String, massSolarMasses : double, luminositySolar : double)</p>
              <p>+ getName() : String</p>
              <p>+ getMassSolarMasses() : double</p>
              <p>+ getLuminositySolar() : double</p>
              <p>+ getHabitableZoneInnerAU() : double</p>
              <p>+ getHabitableZoneOuterAU() : double</p>
              <p>+ isInHabitableZone(orbitalDistanceAU : double) : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <table xml:id="table-project-exoplanet">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>Exoplanet</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>Exoplanet</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- designation : String</p>
              <p>- semiMajorAxisAU : double</p>
              <p>- hostStar : Star</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ Exoplanet(designation : String, semiMajorAxisAU : double, hostStar : Star)</p>
              <p>+ getDesignation() : String</p>
              <p>+ getSemiMajorAxisAU() : double</p>
              <p>+ getHostStar() : Star</p>
              <p>+ calculateOrbitalPeriodYears() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateOrbitalPeriodDays() : double</p>
              <p>+ isPotentiallyHabitable() : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>name</c> and <c>designation</c> must not be null or blank.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>massSolarMasses &gt; 0.0</c> and <c>luminositySolar &gt; 0.0</c>.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>semiMajorAxisAU &gt; 0.0</c> and <c>hostStar != null</c>.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>Exoplanet.isPotentiallyHabitable()</c> delegates directly to
            <c>hostStar.isInHabitableZone(this.semiMajorAxisAU)</c>, demonstrating message passing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write an automated test suite in <c>ExoplanetTest</c> modeling our Sun (<m>1.0 M_\odot, 1.0 L_\odot</m>)
        and Earth (<m>1.0 \text{ AU}</m>), verifying that Earth has an orbital period of approximately
        365.25 days and resides within the habitable zone. Then test Proxima Centauri and Proxima b.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Convert years to days using <c>periodYears * 365.25</c>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 7: WeatherObservation and FlightSafetyAdvisor        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-flight-safety">
    <title>Aviation Flight Category and Crosswind Safety Advisor</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        General aviation pilots rely on METAR weather observations to determine whether flight
        conditions meet FAA flight rules: Visual Flight Rules (VFR), Marginal VFR (MVFR), or
        Instrument Flight Rules (IFR). In addition, pilots must calculate the crosswind component
        relative to the active runway heading:
        <md>V_{\text{cross}} = V_{\text{wind}} \times |\sin(\alpha_{\text{wind}} - \alpha_{\text{runway}})|</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Design and implement two classes: an immutable <c>WeatherObservation</c> and an analytical
        <c>FlightSafetyAdvisor</c>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-weather-obs">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>WeatherObservation</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>WeatherObservation</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- stationId : String</p>
              <p>- visibilityMiles : double</p>
              <p>- cloudCeilingFeet : int</p>
              <p>- windSpeedKnots : double</p>
              <p>- windDirectionDegrees : int</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ WeatherObservation(stationId : String, visibilityMiles : double, cloudCeilingFeet : int, windSpeedKnots : double, windDirectionDegrees : int)</p>
              <p>+ getStationId() : String</p>
              <p>+ getVisibilityMiles() : double</p>
              <p>+ getCloudCeilingFeet() : int</p>
              <p>+ getWindSpeedKnots() : double</p>
              <p>+ getWindDirectionDegrees() : int</p>
              <p>+ determineFlightCategory() : String</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <table xml:id="table-project-flight-advisor">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>FlightSafetyAdvisor</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>FlightSafetyAdvisor</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- maxDemonstratedCrosswindKnots : double</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ FlightSafetyAdvisor(maxDemonstratedCrosswindKnots : double)</p>
              <p>+ getMaxDemonstratedCrosswindKnots() : double</p>
              <p>+ calculateCrosswindComponent(obs : WeatherObservation, runwayHeadingDegrees : int) : double</p>
              <p>+ isSafeToLand(obs : WeatherObservation, runwayHeadingDegrees : int) : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>FAA Category Rules and Class Invariants:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>visibilityMiles &gt;= 0.0</c> and <c>cloudCeilingFeet &gt;= 0</c>.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>windSpeedKnots &gt;= 0.0</c> and <c>windDirectionDegrees</c> between 0 and 360.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>determineFlightCategory()</c> returns:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><c>"VFR"</c> if ceiling &gt;= 3000 ft and visibility &gt;= 5.0 miles.</p></li>
            <li><p><c>"MVFR"</c> if ceiling is 1000–2999 ft or visibility is 3.0–4.9 miles.</p></li>
            <li><p><c>"IFR"</c> if ceiling is &lt; 1000 ft or visibility is &lt; 3.0 miles.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>isSafeToLand()</c> returns <c>true</c> if and only if the flight category is <c>"VFR"</c>
            and the crosswind component does not exceed <c>maxDemonstratedCrosswindKnots</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write unit tests in <c>FlightSafetyTest</c> verifying correct flight category classification
        and crosswind math across multiple runway headings.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Project 8: StormTracker                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="project-storm-tracker">
    <title>Tropical Cyclone Lifecycle and Saffir-Simpson Tracker</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Meteorological organizations track the lifecycle of tropical cyclones as they intensify
        and weaken over the ocean. As new aircraft reconnaissance advisories arrive, the system
        must update the storm's current sustained wind speed, update peak lifetime intensity,
        and classify the storm on the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Tropical Depression: &lt; 34 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Tropical Storm: 34–63 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Category 1 Hurricane: 64–82 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Category 2 Hurricane: 83–95 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Category 3 Major Hurricane: 96–112 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Category 4 Major Hurricane: 113–136 knots</p></li>
        <li><p>Category 5 Major Hurricane: &gt;= 137 knots</p></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Design and implement a stateful tracker class named <c>StormTracker</c>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-project-storm-tracker">
        <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>StormTracker</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="100%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell halign="center"><strong>StormTracker</strong></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>- stormName : String</p>
              <p>- basin : String</p>
              <p>- currentWindSpeedKnots : double</p>
              <p>- peakWindSpeedKnots : double</p>
              <p>- advisoryCount : int</p>
              <p>- active : boolean</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <p>+ StormTracker(stormName : String, basin : String, initialWindSpeedKnots : double)</p>
              <p>+ getStormName() : String</p>
              <p>+ getBasin() : String</p>
              <p>+ getCurrentWindSpeedKnots() : double</p>
              <p>+ getPeakWindSpeedKnots() : double</p>
              <p>+ getAdvisoryCount() : int</p>
              <p>+ isActive() : boolean</p>
              <p>+ recordAdvisory(newWindSpeedKnots : double) : void</p>
              <p>+ markDissipated() : void</p>
              <p>+ getSaffirSimpsonCategory() : String</p>
              <p>+ isMajorHurricane() : boolean</p>
              <p>+ toString() : String</p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p><strong>Class Invariants and Requirements:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><c>stormName</c> and <c>basin</c> must not be null or blank.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>currentWindSpeedKnots &gt;= 0.0</c>.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>peakWindSpeedKnots &gt;= currentWindSpeedKnots</c> at all times.</p></li>
        <li><p><c>advisoryCount &gt;= 1</c>.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>recordAdvisory(newWindSpeedKnots)</c> asserts that the storm <c>isActive()</c>
            and that <c>newWindSpeedKnots &gt;= 0.0</c>. It updates <c>currentWindSpeedKnots</c>,
            increments <c>advisoryCount</c>, and updates <c>peakWindSpeedKnots</c> if the new
            wind speed exceeds the previous peak.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <c>markDissipated()</c> transitions <c>active</c> to <c>false</c>. Subsequent calls to
            <c>recordAdvisory()</c> must fail with an assertion error.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Write an automated test class in <c>StormTrackerTest</c> verifying a simulated lifecycle:
        formation at 30 knots, rapid intensification to a 120-knot Category 4 hurricane,
        weakening to 50 knots (confirming that peak intensity remains 120 knots), dissipation,
        and rejection of post-dissipation advisory updates.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
